1. Introduction
Parkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder and represents one of the fastest-growing neurological causes of disability worldwide. Clinically, PD is characterized by four major motor symptoms: resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability. PD also includes many non-motor symptoms such as cognitive decline, sleep disturbance, mood disorders, and autonomic dysfunctions. Most importantly, motor symptoms of PD develop only after considerable degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra, which greatly diminishes the opportunity for early therapeutic intervention [1,2,3,4].
The global burden of PD is increasing. The estimated number of people living with PD rose from approximately 2.5 million in 1990 to 6.1 million in 2016, and the World Health Organization reported more than 8.5 million cases in 2019 [5,6]. Projections suggest that prevalence may exceed 25 million by 2050 as populations age [7]. In the United States alone, the annual economic burden exceeds USD 50 billion [8]. This growing clinical and socioeconomic burden is especially important because available therapies primarily alleviate symptoms rather than modify the underlying neurodegenerative process [6] [9].
Current diagnostic frameworks rely primarily on clinical examination and rating scales, including the Movement Disorder Society-sponsored revision of the Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale (MDS-UPDRS). Although clinically valuable, these assessments are partly subjective, vulnerable to inter-rater variation, and provide only intermittent snapshots of fluctuating symptoms. Molecular and imaging biomarkers, particularly those related to alpha-synuclein pathology, have shown diagnostic potential [10]; however, no single biomarker currently provides sufficient sensitivity, specificity, accessibility, and validation for population-scale early screening. These limitations motivate the development of data-driven approaches that can integrate complementary clinical, digital, imaging, and molecular information.
Over the past decade, ML and DL methods have been applied to speech [11], handwriting [12], gait and movement [13,14], neuroimaging [15], and longitudinal wearable or mobile sensor data from resources such as mPower [16] and the Parkinson's Progression Markers Initiative (PPMI) [17]. Multimodal models seek to combine complementary behavioural, imaging, physiological, and molecular signals rather than treating each source in isolation [18]. Federated learning may also support multisite model development without centralising all patient-level data [19]. Beyond diagnosis and monitoring, AI has been used in quantitative structure-activity relationship modelling, structure-based drug design, and virtual screening for targets implicated in PD, including alpha-synuclein aggregation and leucine-rich repeat kinase 2 (LRRK2) [20]. These applications differ in data scale, model complexity, interpretability, and clinical maturity; therefore, their reported performance cannot be compared reliably without considering the underlying datasets and validation protocols.
Despite encouraging results, the evidence base has important methodological weaknesses. Many studies use small, single-centre, or modality-specific cohorts and evaluate models only through random within-dataset splits. Such designs can inflate performance when records from the same participant, device, or acquisition environment appear in both training and test sets. Generalisation commonly deteriorates under independent or cross-dataset evaluation because hardware, acquisition protocols, cohort demographics, clinical definitions, and annotation strategies differ across studies. DL can learn complex representations from high-dimensional data, but this advantage is offset by greater data and computational requirements, overfitting risk, and limited clinical interpretability. Multimodal and multisite research introduces additional problems of missing data, harmonisation, privacy, governance, and reproducibility. Moreover, patient-level digital phenotyping and molecular-level drug-discovery research are often developed as separate pipelines, limiting the construction of biologically informed, end-to-end models.
Recent reviews have broadened the discussion of AI for PD. Sharma et al. [21], for example, organised ML and DL evidence across imaging, electroencephalography, voice, gait, handwriting, emotion, biomarkers, public datasets, explainable AI, and multimodal analysis. That review provides a useful methodology-oriented overview, but a gap remains for a dataset-centric synthesis that links cohort characteristics and data accessibility to validation strategy, reported performance, generalisability, and clinical readiness. The relationship between patient-level digital biomarkers and molecular evidence, including proteomics, transcriptomics, multi-omics integration, and AI-assisted drug discovery, also remains fragmented. The present review addresses these gaps by evaluating more than 20 public benchmark datasets across seven biomarker modalities; comparing conventional ML, DL, multimodal, explainable, and privacy-preserving approaches; and examining external validity, interpretability, and clinical translation as cross-cutting criteria. It further integrates digital phenotyping with molecular AI and proposes a conceptual multimodal framework for diagnosis, severity assessment, and progression monitoring. The objective is not merely to catalogue studies, but to identify why apparently strong results may fail to generalise and to define testable priorities for future PD research. Fig. 1 summarises how this analytical scope is organised across the manuscript [22,23,24].
