1.2. Research objectives
Accordingly, this review addresses seven research objectives:
●
Characterise how conventional machine learning, deep learning, multimodal learning, explainable AI, privacy-preserving learning, and generative AI have been applied to PD detection, severity assessment, progression modelling, biomarker discovery, and drug discovery.
●
Evaluate more than 20 publicly available PD benchmark datasets across seven biomarker modalities with respect to cohort size and composition, demographic representation, annotation quality, accessibility, longitudinal structure, and suitability for supervised, semi-supervised, or self-supervised learning.
●
Interpret reported model performance in relation to sample size, class balance, participant-level data separation, validation strategy, comparator selection, and independent or cross-dataset testing, rather than treating accuracy or area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) as directly comparable across studies.
●
Compare early, intermediate, late, and attention-based multimodal fusion strategies, including their potential advantages over unimodal baselines and their requirements for data harmonisation, missing-data handling, computational resources, and interpretability.
●
Examine how genetic, biofluid, proteomic, transcriptomic, and multi-omics data are used for PD biomarker discovery and AI-assisted therapeutic development, with particular attention to alpha-synuclein, leucine-rich repeat kinase 2 (LRRK2), and cross-scale integration with digital phenotypes.
●
Identify barriers to clinical translation, including dataset shift, modality imbalance, limited external validation, poor reproducibility, insufficient interpretability, privacy and governance constraints, regulatory requirements, and integration with clinical workflows.
●
Develop an evidence-informed conceptual framework and a specific research agenda for designing and testing future PD-AI systems, while clearly distinguishing proposed methodological directions from experimentally validated clinical tools.
1.3. Scope of the survey
This review examines AI applications to PD in peer-reviewed journal articles and established conference proceedings published between 2007 and 2026, with emphasis on work published from 2018 onward and retention of earlier foundational studies. The scope covers seven biomarker modalities: speech and voice; neuroimaging, including magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), dopamine transporter imaging (DaTSCAN), positron emission tomography (PET), and single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT); gait and wearable sensing; handwriting and fine-motor assessment; clinical scales and structured clinical data; genetic and biofluid biomarkers; and protein, molecular, and chemical data. The principal applications are PD detection, severity assessment, progression modelling, digital and molecular biomarker discovery, and AI-assisted drug discovery. Cross-cutting methods include multimodal fusion, explainable AI, federated and privacy-preserving learning, generative molecular modelling, foundation models, and clinical decision-support approaches. More than 20 public benchmark datasets are assessed within this scope.
The review does not provide clinical practice guidelines, recommend medications, or replace specialist diagnostic judgement. A statistical meta-analysis is not performed because the included studies differ substantially in populations, modalities, tasks, outcomes, and validation protocols; reported metrics are therefore synthesised comparatively and interpreted in methodological context. Purely deterministic signal-processing methods without a learning component are discussed only when needed to explain preprocessing or feature extraction. Animal and in vitro studies are included only when they directly inform molecular AI or therapeutic-discovery pipelines. Regulatory, privacy, and reimbursement considerations are discussed at the level required to evaluate clinical translation, but the review does not constitute a formal legal, regulatory, or health-economic analysis.
The complete search strategy, databases, search terms, screening stages, duplicate-removal procedure, eligibility criteria, reasons for exclusion, and study-quality appraisal are reported in Section 3 using a Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA)-guided process. Eligible evidence prioritises peer-reviewed studies. Selected preprints are considered only when they describe a highly relevant emerging method or an authoritative dataset and are identified as non-peer-reviewed evidence. This distinction is maintained during interpretation so that preliminary findings are not given the same evidential weight as externally validated peer-reviewed studies.
Fig. 1 presents the high-level analytical structure of the review. Section 2 summarises the clinical and pathological basis of PD and the rationale for AI-assisted assessment. Section 3 reports the systematic review methodology. Section 4 critically compares existing research across behavioural, imaging, multimodal, and molecular applications. Section 5 examines data collection, preprocessing, modalities, and public benchmark datasets. Section 6 compares ML and DL approaches and interprets their reported performance. Section 7 presents the conceptual multimodal case study while explicitly separating illustrative outcomes from experimental results. Section 8 synthesises limitations, clinical-translation challenges, and future research priorities. Section 9 provides the conclusion.
2. Understanding Parkinson's disease detection using AI
Parkinson's disease is a multisystem neurodegenerative disorder characterised by progressive loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta and by pathological alpha-synuclein aggregation, including Lewy-body formation [38]. Its core motor manifestations are bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor, and postural instability. Non-motor features include hyposmia, rapid-eye-movement sleep behaviour disorder, constipation, depression, autonomic dysfunction, and cognitive impairment; several may precede recognisable motor symptoms by years [3,42]. The prodromal phase is therefore important for risk stratification and disease-modification trials, although earlier detection should not be equated with the availability of a proven disease-modifying intervention.
Clinical and biological heterogeneity is a central challenge for both diagnosis and model development. The presence, severity, and rate of progression of symptoms vary across patients and reflect interactions among genetic susceptibility, including LRRK2, SNCA, and GBA variants, environmental exposures, comorbidities, treatment state, and age-related neurobiology [43]. Consequently, a model trained on one cohort or disease stage may learn cohort-specific patterns rather than generalisable PD biology. No single clinical, digital, imaging, or molecular marker currently captures the full disease spectrum; complementary modalities must therefore be evaluated for both incremental information and potential sources of bias [44,4].
PD data span acoustic signals, motion trajectories, neuroimages, wearable measurements, clinical assessments, genetic variants, biofluid markers, and molecular interaction data. Machine learning can model nonlinear relationships within these data, while deep learning can learn representations directly from high-dimensional signals and images. These advantages are conditional: performance depends on sample size, label quality, participant-independent validation, confounder control, and consistency between training and deployment settings. Multimodal integration may provide complementary information, but it also introduces missing-modality, harmonisation, computational, and interpretability challenges. Fig. 2 summarises the progression from modality-specific analysis toward integrated and clinically oriented AI frameworks.
