Tremor is treated as a distinct motor phenotype, not as a component of gait. It is not listed as a separate top-level data modality because tremor measurements are acquired through several existing modality groups: accelerometers and gyroscopes in the gait and wearable category, electromyography or video-based movement signals, and pen trajectories or spiral drawings in the handwriting and fine-motor category [13,16,14,45]. Within these groups, tremor-specific outcomes—including amplitude, dominant frequency, temporal variability, body location, task condition, and medication response must be analysed separately from gait outcomes such as stride length, cadence, postural stability, and freezing of gait. This organisation avoids duplicating datasets while preserving tremor as an explicit clinical and computational target.
Conventional machine-learning methods, including support vector machines, random forests, k-nearest neighbours, and regularised regression, remain useful when datasets are modest and clinically interpretable features can be specified in advance. Their lower computational cost and clearer feature-level attribution can support transparent baselines; however, performance depends strongly on preprocessing, feature selection, and control of confounding variables. Deep-learning architectures relax part of this feature-engineering requirement: convolutional neural networks can learn spatial or spectrotemporal representations, whereas recurrent and temporal-convolutional networks model sequential sensor or speech data. These benefits require larger, well-labelled samples and stronger safeguards against overfitting, data leakage, and site-specific shortcuts. Transformer-based models can capture long-range dependencies and support multimodal or longitudinal modelling, but their parameter count, data demand, calibration, and computational burden may limit their advantage in small PD cohorts. Consequently, architecture complexity should be justified by participant-independent and preferably external validation rather than by within-dataset accuracy alone. Fig. 3 summarises this progression from feature-based models to integrated architectures.
