Privacy-preserving learning can facilitate multi-institutional PD research without transferring all raw patient records to a central repository. Federated learning, for example, enables participating sites to exchange model updates rather than source data [19]. This design may reduce central data exposure, but it does not by itself guarantee privacy, regulatory compliance, or equitable performance. Model updates may leak information unless additional safeguards are used, and differences in devices, acquisition protocols, clinical populations, label definitions, and local sample sizes can destabilise training or produce uneven site-level performance. Secure aggregation, differential privacy, governance agreements, audit trails, and external evaluation across participating and unseen centres are therefore required before federated PD models can be considered clinically dependable.
Clinical severity and progression in Parkinson's disease are commonly characterised using standardised rating instruments. The Movement Disorder Society-sponsored Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale (MDS-UPDRS) contains four parts covering non-motor experiences of daily living, motor experiences of daily living, motor examination, and motor complications [46]. The Hoehn and Yahr (H&Y) scale provides a coarser stage-based description of motor disability and functional dependence. These instruments are clinically meaningful reference measures for supervised modelling, but they should not be described as error-free ground truth. Scores may vary with examiner training, patient self-report, medication state, time of assessment, and the scale's sensitivity to small or short-term changes; the H&Y scale also compresses heterogeneous clinical presentations into a limited number of stages.
AI-based severity models can complement these episodic assessments by extracting repeated or continuous measures from speech, gait, tremor, handwriting, video, smartphones, and wearable sensors. Their potential value lies in quantifying within-person variation between clinic visits and relating digital changes to treatment state or disease trajectory. However, frequent measurement is not automatically clinically valid: models may capture device placement, task adherence, environment, medication timing, or individual behaviour rather than disease progression. Evaluation should therefore report agreement with relevant clinical outcomes, test-retest reliability, sensitivity to meaningful change, calibration, participant-independent validation, and performance across demographic and clinical subgroups. Digital measures should be presented as adjuncts to—not replacements for neurological examination and validated patient- and clinician-reported outcomes. Fig. 4 organises the principal AI method families considered in this review and highlights their different analytical roles.
●
Conventional machine learning: Support vector machines (SVMs), random forests, k-nearest neighbours (KNNs), and regularised regression are appropriate baselines for modest, structured datasets with clinically interpretable features [11,25,27]. They generally require less computation and can support feature-level inspection, but performance is sensitive to preprocessing, feature selection, class imbalance, and participant leakage. Reported accuracy values cannot be compared directly across acoustic or kinematic studies unless cohort composition, task design, and validation strategy are comparable.
●
Unimodal deep learning: Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) learn spatial or spectrotemporal representations, whereas recurrent neural networks (RNNs) and long short-term memory (LSTM) networks model sequential dependencies in speech and sensor data [26,28,30]. These architectures reduce manual feature engineering but usually require larger samples and careful regularisation. High within-dataset performance in neuroimaging or speech studies [29,[30], [47], [48]] may reflect scanner, recording, or participant-specific signals; subject-level splitting and external testing are therefore essential.
●
Multimodal deep learning: Early fusion combines features before modelling, intermediate fusion learns shared representations, and late fusion combines modality-specific predictions; transformer or attention-based approaches can additionally model cross-modal relationships [18,35,36]. Multimodal models may capture complementary disease information, but an apparent improvement over a unimodal baseline is meaningful only when the same participants, validation folds, and missing-data rules are used. Alignment, incomplete modalities, sample-size reduction, and increased computational burden can offset the theoretical benefit of fusion.
●
Privacy-preserving AI: Federated learning, differential privacy, secure aggregation, and secure multiparty computation can support collaborative model development while limiting direct exchange of patient-level data [19,49]. These techniques reduce particular exposure risks but do not automatically ensure compliance with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), or local governance requirements. Privacy guarantees, utility loss, communication cost, site heterogeneity, and performance across institutions must be reported explicitly.
●
Generative and molecular AI: Graph neural networks (GNNs), quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) models, and variational autoencoders (VAEs) support protein-interaction modelling, virtual screening, and candidate generation [[20], [37], [38], [39],40,10]. These methods can prioritise alpha-synuclein aggregation inhibitors or LRRK2-related targets, but computational ranking is an early discovery step rather than evidence of therapeutic efficacy. Experimental confirmation, toxicity assessment, pharmacokinetic evaluation, and clinical validation remain necessary.
●
Explainable AI (XAI): Class activation mapping (CAM), Shapley additive explanations (SHAP), local interpretable model-agnostic explanations (LIME), and attention visualisation can expose influential regions or features [50,41,51]. Explanation quality should not be inferred from visual plausibility alone: attributions may be unstable, method-dependent, or faithful to a biased model. Clinical evaluation should test reproducibility, agreement with domain knowledge, usefulness for error detection, and whether explanations improve decisions rather than merely increase user confidence.
