Search strategy: IEEE Xplore, PubMed, ScienceDirect, SpringerLink, the ACM Digital Library, and Google Scholar were searched for records published from January 2007 through 31 March 2026. Searches covered titles, abstracts, and available keywords. Boolean combinations were constructed around three concept groups: Parkinson's disease; artificial intelligence, machine learning, deep learning, or computational modelling; and application or modality terms including speech, voice, tremor, gait, wearable sensors, handwriting, neuroimaging, MRI, DaTSCAN, digital biomarkers, genetics, biofluids, multimodal learning, explainable AI, federated learning, molecular modelling, and drug discovery. Database-specific syntax was adapted where required, and reference lists of eligible studies and relevant reviews were examined for additional records.
Record management and screening: Retrieved records were combined in a master record set. Duplicate removal was performed first by matching digital object identifiers and then by comparing normalised titles, first authors, and publication years; uncertain matches were resolved by manual inspection. Screening proceeded in two stages: title/abstract review followed by full-text eligibility assessment using the criteria below.
●
Selection procedure: Of 618 identified records, 108 duplicates were removed and 510 titles and abstracts were screened. A total of 360 records were excluded because they were unrelated to PD, lacked an AI component, or did not provide accessible full text. The remaining 150 reports underwent full-text assessment. Seventy-six were excluded: 46 lacked sufficient methodological detail and 30 did not report a quantitative evaluation relevant to the review questions. Seventy-four studies were retained for qualitative synthesis. Fig. 6 reports these stages and exclusion reasons.
