●
Inclusion criteria: Studies were eligible when they (i) were English-language, peer-reviewed full-text articles or established conference papers; (ii) applied AI, machine learning, or deep learning to PD detection, severity estimation, progression modelling, biomarker analysis, or drug discovery; (iii) evaluated at least one prespecified behavioural, clinical, imaging, wearable, genetic, biofluid, protein, or molecular modality; and (iv) reported sufficient methodological and quantitative information for critical interpretation. Selected preprints were considered only as clearly identified emerging evidence and were not treated as equivalent to peer-reviewed validation studies.
●
Exclusion criteria: Records were excluded when they (i) used only conventional statistical analysis without a learning component; (ii) duplicated or substantially overlapped a previously included dataset and analysis without adding relevant evidence; (iii) were editorials, letters, abstracts, protocols, or unavailable full texts; (iv) did not address PD diagnosis, monitoring, progression, biomarkers, or therapeutic discovery; or (v) lacked enough information to evaluate the model, data, or validation procedure. Non-English reports were excluded because translation resources were not available, creating a potential language bias acknowledged in Section 8.
Methodological appraisal and synthesis: Because the evidence spans diagnostic classification, progression modelling, digital sensing, neuroimaging, and molecular discovery, no single formal risk-of-bias instrument was applicable to every study. Instead, each included study was appraised using a common review-specific framework covering dataset size and provenance, class balance, participant-level separation, missing-data handling, feature or model transparency, internal and external validation, metric appropriateness, reproducibility, and clinical relevance. These domains were used to qualify reported performance and identify recurring limitations; no statistical meta-analysis was conducted because populations, modalities, outcomes, and validation protocols were too heterogeneous for defensible pooling. The final corpus comprised 74 studies across seven modality groups and more than 20 benchmark datasets. The following PRISMA flowchart summarises the paper selection procedure as shown in Fig. 6.
