The transition from descriptive clinical observation to computational analysis reflects a broader change in how Parkinson's disease is studied. Historical descriptions established tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and gait disturbance as recognisable clinical manifestations [1,2], whereas contemporary research treats PD as a heterogeneous multisystem disorder requiring behavioural, imaging, clinical, and molecular evidence. AI is relevant because these data are high-dimensional and partly complementary; however, algorithmic analysis does not remove uncertainty in diagnosis, labels, or treatment response. Table 1 therefore compares representative studies by modality, model and task, reported evaluation, validation limitations, and clinical readiness rather than ranking them solely by headline performance.
1.
Speech and voice: Illustrates why high reported classification performance must be interpreted cautiously. Feature-based SVM and random-forest models are efficient for small acoustic datasets [11,25,27], whereas aggregation and deep-learning approaches can reduce sensitivity to individual utterances [26]. However, repeated recordings from the same participant must remain within a single validation fold; otherwise, a model can identify speakers rather than PD. Language, sex, age, microphone, task, medication state, and recording environment also alter acoustic distributions. Cross-dataset results reported in recent reviews show that performance can fall substantially when models are transferred between voice corpora [21]. Speech is therefore promising for low-cost screening and monitoring, but current evidence supports an adjunctive research role rather than stand-alone diagnosis.
2.
Neuroimaging: MRI and DaTSCAN provide anatomically and physiologically relevant information but create a different validation problem. Studies use conventional classifiers, CNNs, 3D CNNs, attention mechanisms, and region-focused architectures [15,17,[28], [29], [32],[30], [47], [48]]. Multicentre datasets can improve heterogeneity, yet random participant splitting does not guarantee transportability when scanner manufacturer, field strength, preprocessing, or site composition differ. Site-stratified or leave-one-centre-out evaluation is more informative than pooled internal accuracy. Interpretability is also modality-specific: a salient brain region is not necessarily a disease mechanism, and models may exploit acquisition artefacts. Imaging AI is therefore best evaluated as a specialist decision-support adjunct requiring external-centre and prospective workflow validation.
3.
Gait, tremor, wearables, and smartphones: These modalities offer repeated measurements outside the clinic and may reveal fluctuations missed by episodic examinations [13,16,14]. Their principal strength is ecological and temporal coverage, not inherently higher diagnostic accuracy. Real-world signals are affected by sensor placement, device generation, walking surface, footwear, medication timing, comorbidity, adherence, and missingness. Large remote cohorts such as mPower increase scale but may rely on self-reported labels and non-random participation [16]. Reliable evaluation therefore requires participant-level separation, device- and site-stratified testing, comparison with clinically meaningful change, and reporting of data loss and adherence. These modalities are currently more defensible for monitoring defined symptoms than for replacing neurological diagnosis.
4.
Spatio-temporal graph convolutional networks (ST-GCNs): These are an emerging approach for representing body joints as graph nodes and skeletal connections as edges, enabling joint modelling of spatial relationships and movement over time [[52], [53], [54], [55], [56]]. This inductive structure may be useful for gait deviation, postural instability, and freezing-of-gait analysis, but greater architectural sophistication does not resolve small cohorts, inconsistent pose estimation, occlusion, class imbalance, or protocol shift. Comparisons with simpler temporal baselines should use identical participant splits and report computational demand, calibration, and external performance. Evidence for ST-GCN-based PD assessment remains predominantly retrospective; prospective evaluation in unconstrained clinical and home environments is still required.
5.
Handwriting and fine-motor assessment: Digitised handwriting and drawing provide low-cost measures of pen pressure, velocity, acceleration, in-air time, and stroke geometry [12,33,34]. Their clinical interpretability is attractive because the tasks probe bradykinesia and tremor directly; nevertheless, performance is highly dependent on the tablet, sampling rate, task instructions, handedness, medication state, and feature-extraction pipeline. Image-based CNNs can also learn page layout or acquisition artefacts rather than motor physiology if images from the same participant occur in both training and test sets. Participant-level splitting, device-independent testing, and comparison with established motor scores are therefore necessary. Current evidence supports handwriting as a complementary motor assessment, not an independent diagnostic test.
6.
Multimodal learning and cohort-scale modelling: Combining speech, imaging, gait, clinical, and genetic data can represent complementary aspects of PD [18,35,36], but improved performance is not guaranteed by adding modalities. Multimodal cohorts are usually smaller than their unimodal source datasets because only participants with complete matched measurements can be included. This creates selection bias, while missing-modality imputation can introduce further uncertainty. Fusion models should therefore be compared with strong unimodal baselines on identical participants and validation folds, and should report the incremental value, calibration, missing-data strategy, computational cost, and interpretability of each modality. Until externally validated, multimodal gains should be interpreted as evidence of complementary signal rather than proof of clinical superiority.
7.
Molecular biomarkers and AI-assisted drug discovery: Structure-based ML, quantitative structure-activity relationship modelling, and virtual screening can prioritise alpha-synuclein or LRRK2-related candidates [[20], [37], [38], [39],40]. These approaches reduce the experimental search space, but docking scores, binding predictions, and screening efficiency are intermediate computational outcomes rather than evidence of therapeutic efficacy. Candidate ranking remains sensitive to structural assumptions, assay composition, chemical-space bias, and data leakage between related compounds. Prospective wet-laboratory confirmation, toxicity and pharmacokinetic testing, and ultimately clinical evaluation are required. Molecular AI should therefore be presented as a preclinical prioritisation tool rather than a demonstrated treatment-discovery endpoint.
4.1. Proteomics, transcriptomics, and multi-omics in AI-driven PD research
Molecular omics can complement patient-level behavioural and imaging biomarkers by describing biological heterogeneity in PD. The most relevant evidence clusters into three areas: alpha-synuclein seed-amplification and proteomic assays; single-cell or single-nucleus transcriptomic atlases; and integrative models combining genomic, epigenomic, proteomic, imaging, and clinical data. These sources operate at different biological resolutions and cannot be treated as interchangeable predictors. Their value depends on assay reproducibility, cohort representativeness, phenotype linkage, and validation in independent samples. Accordingly, this section evaluates what each omics layer contributes, which biases accompany its measurement, and whether reported associations support diagnosis, stratification, progression modelling, or only hypothesis generation.
Alpha-synuclein seed-amplification assays (SAAs) in cerebrospinal fluid and blood-derived material are among the most clinically relevant molecular developments in PD. PPMI analyses report high discrimination between PD and healthy controls and suggest biologically distinct subgroups [57], while neuronal extracellular-vesicle approaches may enable less invasive or prodromal assessment [58]. AI-assisted mass-spectrometry studies have also reported plasma proteomic signatures with held-out AUCs above 0.9 [59]. These results should not be compared directly, however, because matrices, assay platforms, case definitions, disease stages, and control groups differ. A high held-out AUC within one cohort does not establish calibration, differential-diagnosis performance, or transportability. Multicentre prospective validation with prespecified thresholds is required before these biomarkers can support routine diagnosis.
Single-nucleus RNA sequencing of post-mortem substantia nigra has identified vulnerable dopaminergic-neuron subtypes, glial activation patterns, and previously under-characterised cell populations associated with PD-risk genes [[60], [61], [62]]. Machine learning can organise these high-dimensional profiles using feature selection, matrix factorisation, topic models, or neural networks, while bulk and integrated RNA-seq analyses identify recurrent pathways involving synaptic and mitochondrial dysfunction, neuroinflammation, and protein homeostasis [63]. The evidence is primarily mechanistic and retrospective: post-mortem tissue represents late disease, cell recovery and dropout are non-random, batch and ancestry effects can dominate biological signal, and cell-level observations are not independent patient samples. These atlases therefore strengthen biological hypotheses but do not yet constitute validated patient-level diagnostic models.
Multi-omics integration combines partially complementary layers, including methylation, gene expression, proteomics, imaging, cerebrospinal-fluid measures, and clinical phenotypes. Sparse PLS-DA, DIABLO, network analysis, gradient boosting, and temporal matrix-factorisation frameworks have been applied to PPMI and experimental disease models [[64], [65], [66]]. Such integration may improve stratification or progression modelling, but claims of added value require comparison on the same participants, because complete-case filtering can create a smaller and less representative cohort. Feature selection must be nested within validation, batch correction must not use test information, and external evaluation must preserve the full preprocessing pipeline. These studies describe an emerging framework for diagnosis and therapeutic-target prioritisation, but prospective clinical utility remains unestablished.
Proteomic, transcriptomic, and integrated-omics studies share several limitations. Molecular cohorts are often smaller than imaging or voice datasets; SomaScan, Olink, and label-free mass spectrometry have different measurement ranges and batch effects; and somatic, germline, bulk-tissue, and single-cell signals answer different biological questions. Post-mortem scarcity, dropout, missing modalities, resolution mismatch, limited longitudinal sampling, and weak linkage between molecular signatures and in-vivo phenotypes further restrict generalisability. Priorities are therefore standardised assay and preprocessing protocols, diverse public biofluid cohorts, preregistered external validation, and cross-modal models that quantify uncertainty rather than merely concatenate molecular and phenotypic features.
Across modalities, model choice should follow the clinical question and data-generating process rather than perceived algorithmic sophistication. Acoustic models require speaker-independent validation; neuroimaging requires scanner- and site-aware evaluation; handwriting, gait, tremor, and wearable models require participant-, task-, and device-aware splits; molecular models require scaffold- or assay-aware testing; and multimodal models require matched-cohort baselines and explicit missing-data analysis [11,25,12,13,[20], [26], [37], [38], [39],[10], [18], [35], [40],[14], [16], [17], [27], [33], [41],36]. Federated learning can support distributed analysis [19,41], but it does not itself correct site imbalance, inconsistent labels, or domain shift. This comparison shows that validation design, not headline architecture, is the principal determinant of credible performance.
The recurring weaknesses are small or selectively sampled cohorts, class imbalance, repeated-measure leakage, inconsistent preprocessing, absent calibration, limited external validation, and incomplete reporting of demographic and device characteristics. These problems explain why high internal accuracy may not translate across languages, scanners, sensors, assays, institutions, or clinical populations. Reproducible PD AI therefore requires participant-level separation, transparent preprocessing, clinically relevant comparators, uncertainty estimates, subgroup analysis, and independent prospective testing. Privacy-preserving or computationally complex frameworks should additionally report communication cost, privacy-utility trade-offs, failure modes, and governance requirements [19,41].
5. Parkinson's disease data collection and preprocessing
Parkinson's disease research combines heterogeneous, high-dimensional data rather than one uniformly 'big' dataset. Volume arises from imaging, continuous sensor streams, sequencing, and molecular profiling; variety spans clinical scores, audio, movement, images, and omics; velocity is most relevant to longitudinal smartphone and wearable monitoring; and veracity is constrained by sensor noise, missingness, diagnostic uncertainty, and inconsistent clinical annotations [67,68]. These properties make data provenance, preprocessing, storage, and validation part of the scientific method. In particular, preprocessing performed before participant-level splitting can leak information, and harmonisation fitted on pooled sites can inflate generalisation. The central challenge is therefore not only computational scale, but preserving clinically meaningful signals while preventing leakage, bias, and site-specific shortcuts.
