Speech and voice recordings offer scalable, non-invasive measurement of phonation, articulation, and prosody [11,25,26,27]. Their low acquisition cost is a strength, but sustained vowels, read speech, and spontaneous speech capture different tasks and cannot be pooled without accounting for language, microphone, environment, medication state, and repeated speakers. Speech models are therefore most credible when evaluated with participant-independent and cross-corpus testing and positioned as screening or monitoring adjuncts rather than stand-alone diagnostic tools.
Structural and functional MRI and SPECT-based DaTSCAN provide complementary anatomical, connectivity, and dopaminergic information [15,17]. They do not provide interchangeable labels: DaTSCAN supports assessment of presynaptic dopaminergic deficit but does not by itself establish idiopathic PD, while MRI findings can be subtle and protocol-dependent. PPMI improves protocol consistency, yet multicentre transportability still requires scanner-aware harmonisation and site-held-out validation. Imaging AI is therefore better framed as specialist decision support for diagnosis, staging, or differential assessment than as automated confirmation.
Gait, tremor, and other sensor-based motor measures should be distinguished analytically. Gait reflects locomotor timing, balance, and postural control, whereas tremor is an oscillatory symptom that can occur at rest, during posture or action, and independently of walking. Wearables and smartphones can capture both, along with bradykinesia and activity patterns [13,89,90,16]. Their repeated real-world measurements support symptom monitoring and medication-response analysis, but device placement, adherence, context, missingness, and self-reported labels limit comparability. Thus, tremor is included within the sensor/motion family for collection purposes, not considered part of gait [[91], [92], [93], [94]].
Digitised handwriting and drawing quantify pressure, velocity, timing, and trajectory features affected by bradykinesia and tremor [12,33]. These tasks are inexpensive and clinically intuitive, but results depend on the tablet, stylus, sampling rate, instructions, handedness, and task design. Participant-level separation and cross-device replication are required before handwriting performance can be generalised beyond a benchmark dataset [95].
Genetic data identify susceptibility loci and pathways, including LRRK2, SNCA, and GBA, and may support risk stratification or target prioritisation [38,43,17]. Predictive utility is constrained by ancestry imbalance, population structure, incomplete penetrance, and the difference between inherited risk and current clinical state. Models should therefore be evaluated across ancestries and should not present genetic association as deterministic diagnosis. Structure-based drug discovery is a related but preclinical application that requires experimental confirmation.
Clinical scales such as the UPDRS and Hoehn and Yahr staging remain essential outcomes and labels, but they are not error-free ground truth. Inter-rater variability, medication state, visit timing, missing follow-up, and changes in assessment practice can affect supervised-learning targets. Longitudinal cohorts such as PPMI are valuable for progression modelling [1,2,89], provided that repeated visits from one participant remain within the same validation partition and clinically meaningful change is distinguished from measurement noise.
Biofluid biomarkers measure molecular processes such as alpha-synuclein aggregation, inflammation, and neuronal injury. Their biological proximity to disease mechanisms is valuable, but assay platform, sample handling, batch effects, disease stage, and control selection influence reported performance. Integrating biofluids with genetics, imaging, or clinical data may improve biological stratification, yet the incremental value must be demonstrated in matched participants with external validation rather than inferred from multimodal complexity.
Table 2 therefore demonstrates complementarity rather than a hierarchy of modalities. No single source captures diagnosis, severity, progression, and biological subtype simultaneously. Multimodal integration is justified when it answers a defined clinical question and adds validated information beyond simpler baselines; otherwise, it can amplify missingness, selection bias, and implementation burden. Standardised collection, transparent provenance, participant-level splitting, and external validation remain prerequisites for reproducible comparison.
