Structural and chemical databases such as the RCSB Protein Data Bank, AlphaFold DB, ChEMBL, ZINC, and BindingDB support target prioritisation, molecular representation learning, virtual screening, and compound ranking [[20], [37], [38], [39],40]. Their scale reflects structures, predictions, assays, or compounds rather than PD participants. Database overlap, related molecular scaffolds, uncertain predicted structures, assay heterogeneity, and temporal leakage can inflate retrospective evaluation. Molecular benchmarks should therefore use scaffold- or time-aware splits and prospective experimental confirmation; reproducible screening is not equivalent to validated therapeutic efficacy.
Across public PD datasets, the dominant limitations are small or selective cohorts, ancestry and socioeconomic imbalance, underrepresentation of prodromal and atypical parkinsonism, inconsistent diagnostic criteria, incomplete follow-up, repeated-measure leakage, and limited linkage between modalities. Most resources were designed for a narrow task, so they cannot by themselves evaluate an end-to-end multimodal clinical workflow [[10], [18], [35]]. Moreover, frequent reuse of the same benchmark can make incremental architecture comparisons appear more certain than they are.
Next-generation benchmarks should combine diverse multicentre cohorts with standardised core variables while retaining site and cohort provenance. Prespecified participant-level splits, hidden external test sets, calibration, subgroup reporting, clinically meaningful baselines, and common evaluation code are more important than dataset volume alone. Federated learning may enable distributed collaboration, but it does not correct biased recruitment, inconsistent labels, or domain shift; foundation models likewise require independent evaluation and transparent pretraining data [[19], [35], [90]]. Privacy, consent, governance, and re-identification risk must be evaluated alongside predictive performance.
In summary, benchmark datasets enable reproducibility only when their sampling units, labels, preprocessing, and validation boundaries are explicit. Progress requires not merely larger repositories, but datasets that represent real clinical populations and evaluation frameworks that test transportability across language, device, site, cohort, and time.
5.3. Data preprocessing and feature representation
Preprocessing converts raw speech, movement, imaging, handwriting, clinical, and molecular observations into model inputs, but it is also a potential source of bias and information leakage. Filtering, smoothing, normalisation, artefact correction, resampling, segmentation, imputation, and region or event detection may improve signal quality, yet each operation embeds assumptions about which variation is noise and which is disease-related. Every data-dependent step, including scaling, feature selection, imputation, harmonisation, and augmentation, must be fitted within the training partition and then applied unchanged to validation and test data. Reporting only the final model without the preprocessing pipeline prevents reproducibility and can exaggerate performance.
Modality-specific preprocessing should be justified rather than applied as a fixed recipe. Speech pipelines may use denoising, silence removal, amplitude normalisation, segmentation, and spectral representations [11,25,26,69,70], but aggressive filtering can remove weak dysarthric information and corpus-specific voice-activity rules can create domain shift. Augmentation must not produce related versions of one participant in different folds. Speaker identity, language, microphone, task, and recording environment should remain traceable throughout evaluation.
Neuroimaging pipelines commonly include motion correction, skull stripping, registration, intensity normalisation, and segmentation; scanner harmonisation must be learned without test-site information. Gait, tremor, and wearable pipelines require calibration, temporal alignment, filtering, windowing, and missingness handling, with windows from one participant kept in the same fold. Handwriting preprocessing should preserve clinically relevant timing and pressure signals while controlling tablet and task effects. Molecular pipelines require assay quality control, batch correction, normalisation, and careful handling of related compounds, genes, or patient samples. These choices can alter the apparent disease signal and must be included in sensitivity analyses.
Feature representation also changes the evidence being tested. Conventional ML uses prespecified acoustic, kinematic, radiomic, clinical, or molecular descriptors, which can be efficient and interpretable but sensitive to feature engineering. Deep learning learns representations from spectrograms, voxel grids, time-series tensors, images, or molecular graphs, but its larger capacity increases the need for independent data and regularisation. Multimodal systems additionally require temporal alignment, unit harmonisation, missing-modality strategies, and matched-participant baselines. Preprocessing and representation should therefore be evaluated as components of the model, with ablation studies and locked external pipelines rather than treated as neutral preliminary steps.
