Table 4 shows that conventional models remain useful when datasets are modest and features are clinically motivated. SVMs and random forests are computationally efficient and can offer clearer feature attribution than high-capacity deep networks. Their apparent advantage may disappear, however, when feature selection is not nested within cross-validation, repeated observations, cross folds, or hyperparameters are tuned on the test set. Model selection should therefore consider calibration, external performance, sensitivity to preprocessing, and clinical consequences of false positives and false negatives, not accuracy alone.
●
Speech-based modelling approaches: Conventional speech models use jitter, shimmer, harmonic-to-noise measures, cepstral coefficients, and related acoustic descriptors with SVMs, random forests, or nearest-neighbour methods [11,25,26,27,69,70]. They are efficient for small datasets, but their features are influenced by age, sex, language, microphone, task, medication state, and comorbidity. CNN or CNN-RNN models can learn spectral and temporal representations from waveforms or spectrograms [26,71], yet greater capacity increases speaker memorisation and corpus dependence. Comparisons must use identical speaker-independent folds and external corpora; otherwise, higher accuracy cannot be attributed confidently to the architecture. Current evidence supports low-cost adjunctive screening or monitoring, not stand-alone diagnosis.
●
Neuroimaging-based modelling approaches: Two-dimensional CNNs analyse selected MRI slices efficiently, whereas three-dimensional CNNs preserve volumetric context but require more data, memory, and scanner harmonisation [28,36]. Region-focused models may improve anatomical plausibility, while whole-brain models can capture distributed effects but are more vulnerable to site and preprocessing shortcuts. Reported gains from residual, dense, or attention architectures cannot be attributed to architecture alone when cohorts, regions of interest, preprocessing, and validation schemes differ [32,36]. MRI and DaTSCAN also answer different clinical questions, and neither should be treated as a stand-alone confirmation of idiopathic PD. Clinically credible imaging models therefore require participant-level evaluation, scanner- and centre-held-out testing, calibration, and evidence that predictions remain useful beyond demographic and acquisition covariates.
●
Handwriting and fine-motor models: Handcrafted kinematic features such as velocity, acceleration, pressure, jerk, and stroke smoothness can be interpretable and data-efficient, whereas CNN-based image models learn spatial patterns from spirals, meanders, or written text [12,33,45]. Image-only models may discard timing and pressure information and can learn page layout, tablet, or task artefacts. Conversely, online-signal models depend on sampling rate and device calibration. Because the same participant often completes multiple tasks, splitting by image or trial rather than participant can markedly inflate accuracy. Handwriting is therefore best supported as a low-cost complementary motor assessment; cross-device, cross-task, and externally recruited validation are needed before diagnostic claims are justified [75,76].
●
Gait, wearable, and time-series models: Classical models use engineered stride, force, frequency, or variability features, while RNNs, LSTMs, temporal convolutional networks, transformers, and spatiotemporal graph convolutional networks learn sequential or skeletal representations [13,16,14,[52], [53], [54], [55], [56]]. Higher-capacity models can capture freezing of gait and postural dynamics but require larger, consistently sampled datasets and may learn participant, device, or laboratory signatures. Laboratory force-plate data provide precise measurement but limited ecological validity; smartphones and wearables improve longitudinal coverage while introducing adherence, placement, missingness, and firmware effects [16,74]. Evaluation should therefore separate participants and recording periods, report event-level class imbalance and false-alarm burden, and test transportability across devices and free-living settings. Current evidence is strongest for symptom monitoring and event detection, not autonomous diagnosis.
●
Multimodal learning frameworks: Combining voice, imaging, gait, wearables, clinical examinations, genetics, and molecular biomarkers may improve prediction only when modalities are available for matched participants and add information beyond strong unimodal and clinical baselines [18,35,36]. Apparent gains can otherwise reflect a larger feature space, selective inclusion of complete cases, or leakage between correlated visits. Multimodal studies should report the number of participants contributing each modality combination, missing-data mechanisms, modality ablations, calibration, and performance when one or more inputs are unavailable. Clinical value depends on incremental utility relative to acquisition cost, burden, and workflow, not simply higher internal accuracy [99,100].
●
Fusion strategies for multimodal Parkinson's disease analysis: Early fusion is simple and can model cross-modal associations, but it is sensitive to scale differences, high dimensionality, and missing values. Intermediate fusion learns modality-specific representations before combining them through shared embeddings, attention, graphs, or transformers; it can capture interactions but has high data and computation requirements and may be difficult to interpret. Late fusion combines modality-specific predictions and is more modular when inputs are absent, although it may miss low-level interactions [18,35,36,73]. No strategy is universally superior. Comparisons require identical cohorts, preprocessing, validation partitions, parameter-budget controls, unimodal ablations, and evaluation under realistic missing-modality patterns. Otherwise, a reported fusion advantage cannot be separated from differences in data availability or model capacity.
●
Explainable artificial intelligence for Parkinson's disease analysis: SHAP and LIME can describe local feature contributions, while Grad-CAM and Integrated Gradients highlight influential image regions [19,90,32,36]. These methods do not by themselves establish causal biomarkers, biological validity, fairness, or safe clinical reasoning. Explanations can be unstable across model retraining, correlated features, background choices, and preprocessing, and attention weights should not automatically be interpreted as explanations. Evaluation should therefore test explanation stability, sensitivity to clinically irrelevant perturbations, agreement with domain knowledge, subgroup consistency, and usefulness to clinicians. XAI is most defensible as an audit and communication layer accompanying calibrated performance and uncertainty, not as a substitute for external validation.
●
Multi-omics fusion models: Genomic, transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, imaging, and clinical data can support molecular subtyping and progression research, but multi-omics cohorts often have far fewer matched participants than measured features [37,38,43,10]. Cross-attention and transformer-based fusion can represent nonlinear associations, yet their capacity increases overfitting risk and does not resolve batch effects, ancestry imbalance, missing assays, or tissue and time-point mismatch [35,36,73]. Valid studies should use participant-matched inputs, assay-aware quality control, nested feature selection, cohort-held-out validation, and ablations against single-omics and clinical baselines. PPMI and AMP-PD improve access to harmonised resources [17,73], but harmonisation does not eliminate cohort provenance or guarantee that molecular signatures generalise to independent populations.
●
Molecular and protein-structure modelling: QSAR, docking, virtual screening, protein representation learning, and structure-based deep learning can prioritise PD-related targets or compounds involving alpha-synuclein, LRRK2, mitochondrial pathways, and inflammation [[77], [78], [101]]. However, performance on ChEMBL, ZINC, DrugBank, or predicted-structure benchmarks measures ranking or affinity-related tasks, not patient diagnosis or therapeutic efficacy. Random molecular splits can place related scaffolds in training and testing, while database overlap and uncertain predicted structures can exaggerate retrospective performance. Scaffold- or time-aware evaluation, prospective biochemical assays, toxicity and pharmacokinetic assessment, and eventual in-vivo and clinical validation are required. These models therefore support hypothesis generation and preclinical prioritisation rather than direct clinical diagnosis.
