6.1. Critical analysis and comparative evaluation
Across the reviewed evidence, five recurring problems limit inference: overfitting to controlled benchmarks, insufficient statistical reporting, imbalance in the modalities studied, weak baseline comparisons, and inconsistent evaluation metrics. These problems interact. Small datasets increase variance; flexible models amplify leakage and tuning risk; selective metric reporting obscures calibration and error costs; and modality imbalance directs attention toward accessible benchmarks rather than clinically decisive evidence. The following appraisal therefore interprets Table 4, Table 5 as contextual summaries rather than a ranking of algorithms.
6.1.1. Overfitting of controlled benchmarks
Reported accuracies of approximately 85-97% across speech, imaging, and handwriting should be interpreted in relation to cohort size and sampling unit. The UCI Parkinson's speech resource contains repeated recordings from 23 participants with PD and 8 healthy controls, while PaHaW contains 38 participants with PD and 37 controls [69,70,75]. If recordings, images, windows, or visits from the same participant cross folds, a model can recognise participant- or device-specific patterns rather than disease. Repeated reuse of a benchmark also encourages implicit tuning to its acquisition and annotation conventions. Consequently, high internal accuracy may represent benchmark fit rather than transportable diagnostic evidence.
Independent external validation remains uncommon in the summarized evidence, and performance often decreases when acquisition hardware, clinical protocols, language, site, or population changes [29,30]. This domain shift should be quantified using centre-, device-, language-, or cohort-held-out evaluation rather than inferred from random splits. Benchmark results are therefore not upper-bound estimates of clinical utility; they are conditional estimates under a specific sampling and validation design. Generalisable claims require prospective testing on independently recruited participants and reporting of calibration and subgroup performance.
6.1.2. Insufficient statistical rigor
Statistical reporting is inconsistent across the literature. A single train-test split can produce unstable estimates in small cohorts, while ordinary cross-validation remains optimistic if feature selection, preprocessing, augmentation, or hyperparameter tuning is performed outside the training fold. Accuracy or AUC without participant counts, confidence intervals, variance across repeats, class prevalence, or threshold-specific errors does not support robust model comparison. Future studies should use participant-level nested resampling where appropriate, preserve a final untouched test set, report confidence intervals and repeated-run variability, and compare paired predictions with suitable statistical tests [50,51]. Table 4, Table 5 summarize different studies and therefore should not be used as a meta-analysis or as evidence of statistically significant superiority.
6.1.3. Modality imbalance in the literature
The reviewed literature is concentrated in speech and neuroimaging, partly because public benchmarks lower the cost of entry. This accessibility accelerates method development but can create a feedback loop in which frequently reused datasets attract further architecture studies while biofluid, genetic, longitudinal, and multimodal cohorts remain comparatively underexamined. Publication counts therefore reflect data availability and research convenience as well as clinical importance. A balanced agenda should prioritise modalities according to the clinical question, required invasiveness, biological proximity, longitudinal value, and feasibility of independent validation rather than benchmark availability alone.
Biofluid and molecular markers may provide evidence closer to PD biology, including alpha-synuclein-related and inflammatory processes [10,17], but invasive collection, assay variability, batch effects, limited matched cohorts, and uncertain reference standards constrain AI development. Molecular and protein models are also often disconnected from patient-level phenotypes and clinical outcomes. Progress requires shared assay standards, diverse multicentre cohorts, matched clinical and molecular measurements, transparent missingness and batch correction, and prospective confirmation. Increasing dataset size without these design controls would not resolve the evidence gap.
6.1.4. Absence of strong classical baselines in deep learning studies
Deep-learning studies do not always compare against well-tuned classical models under identical conditions. For example, handwriting and neuroimaging architectures in Table 5 report high performance, but differences in preprocessing, augmentation, splits, and optimisation prevent attribution of gains to the neural architecture alone [29,34,30]. A fair baseline must use the same participants, input information, test set, and tuning budget. It should include simple clinical or demographic predictors where relevant, as well as regularised linear models, SVMs, or tree-based methods. Without these controls, additional complexity may improve benchmark fit without improving generalisation or clinical utility.
Conventional models can be computationally efficient and easier to audit, particularly when sample sizes are modest and features are clinically motivated. Deep models may be preferable for high-dimensional images, raw waveforms, or long sequences, but only when their incremental benefit survives ablation, nested tuning, and external testing. Studies should report parameter counts, training cost, inference latency, missing-data behaviour, calibration, and paired performance against strong baselines. The relevant question is not whether deep learning is newer, but whether it adds reliable information at an acceptable operational and clinical cost [50,35].
6.1.5. Evaluation metric inconsistency
Evaluation metrics vary because tasks differ: classification, severity estimation, event detection, longitudinal forecasting, and molecular screening do not share a single outcome. Accuracy can be misleading under class imbalance; AUC-ROC measures ranking but not calibration or benefit at a clinical threshold; and sensitivity and specificity depend on the chosen operating point. Event-detection studies additionally require false alarms per unit time and temporal tolerance, regression requires absolute error and calibration against clinical scales, and molecular screening requires prospective enrichment or assay confirmation. Cross-study comparisons are defensible only within the same task, population, endpoint definition, and validation protocol.
No single metric should be mandated for all PD AI studies. Binary classification should report sensitivity, specificity, positive and negative predictive values at prespecified thresholds, AUC-ROC, precision-recall AUC when classes are imbalanced, and calibration. Severity or progression models should report MAE or RMSE together with uncertainty and correlation to established scales, while longitudinal models require time-aware validation. Decision-curve or utility analysis can show whether predictions improve a defined clinical action. Standardised reporting and shared challenge protocols, including independent test sets and complete participant flow, would improve comparability more than selecting one universal score [89].
Overall, the literature demonstrates technical feasibility across several PD-related tasks, but the certainty of clinical benefit remains limited by benchmark dependence, incomplete statistical reporting, weak comparator design, and heterogeneous endpoints. More credible evidence will require prespecified participant-level analyses, transparent preprocessing, strong baselines, external multicentre validation, calibration, subgroup evaluation, and task-appropriate metrics. These requirements inform the translational challenges and future priorities discussed in the following sections.
7. Illustrative case study: conceptual multimodal AI framework for Parkinson's disease assessment
Fig. 11 presents an illustrative workflow for integrating speech, neuroimaging, handwriting, gait and wearable signals, genetics, biofluids, and molecular representations in PD research [18,35,36]. This is a conceptual case study, not an implemented experiment or validated diagnostic system. Its purpose is to specify the intended application, processing stages, outputs, and evaluation requirements of a multimodal pipeline without implying that the framework has produced patient-level results. The proposed application is decision support for three distinct research tasks: PD-versus-control classification, severity estimation, and longitudinal progression-risk modelling. These tasks would require separate cohorts, endpoints, and validation protocols.
The input stage uses modality-specific processing while preserving participant identity and acquisition provenance. Speech processing may include denoising, voice-activity detection, and acoustic or learned representations [11,26,69]. MRI and DaTSCAN require modality-appropriate correction, registration, normalisation, and region or representation extraction [17,[28], [29], [30], [31], [32], [34], [36], [47], [48], [102]]. Handwriting and gait pipelines may use kinematic, spatial, temporal, or frequency-domain features [12,13,16,14,33,45]. All preprocessing parameters must be learned within training data, and repeated recordings, images, windows, or visits from one participant must remain in the same validation partition. The diagram therefore represents data-flow options rather than a single mandatory preprocessing recipe.
Genetic and biofluid inputs require assay quality control, ancestry and batch-effect assessment, missing-data handling, and feature reduction that is nested within model validation [37,38,43,10,17]. Molecular descriptors, graphs, or protein representations may support target or compound prioritisation [20,38,40,10], but these inputs are not patient-level diagnostic evidence unless they are linked to matched clinical participants and outcomes. Molecular screening should therefore be evaluated separately with scaffold- or time-aware splits and prospective assays before any connection to therapeutic benefit is proposed.
The fusion layer may use early, intermediate, or late integration. A defensible implementation would compare these strategies on the same matched cohort, include strong unimodal and clinical baselines, report which participants contribute each modality combination, and test performance when inputs are missing [18,35,36]. Outputs must be task-specific: classification should be calibrated at prespecified thresholds; severity estimates should be compared with clinical scales using error and uncertainty; and progression models require time-aware validation. The conceptual framework does not assume that fusion will improve performance; incremental value must be demonstrated through ablation and external testing.
XAI methods such as SHAP, LIME, Grad-CAM, and Integrated Gradients may help audit feature contributions [19,90], but their outputs should not be interpreted as causal biomarkers. Explanation stability, clinically irrelevant shortcuts, subgroup consistency, and agreement with domain knowledge must be examined. Uncertainty estimates should identify low-confidence or out-of-distribution cases and support referral to a clinician rather than automate a diagnosis. Any longitudinal updating procedure would require version control, drift monitoring, recalibration, and prospective governance; a model should not self-update in clinical use without controlled revalidation.
Case-study outcome, application, result, and observations: The outcome of this conceptual exercise is a testable workflow and reporting checklist, not a diagnostic accuracy value. Its intended application is multimodal research and clinician-facing decision support. No experimental result is reported because the framework has not been implemented. The principal observations are that matched-participant data, participant-level splitting, missing-modality analysis, unimodal ablations, calibration, uncertainty, external multicentre validation, and workflow evaluation are prerequisites for judging whether multimodal integration adds clinical value. These limitations are stated explicitly to avoid presenting an illustrative architecture as validated evidence.
8. Issues, challenges, and future needs
AI research in PD has expanded across speech, wearables, neuroimaging, handwriting, clinical scales, and molecular data, but technical feasibility has not yet established routine clinical benefit. The main barriers are fragmented evidence, sparse longitudinal follow-up, domain shift, demographic and cohort bias, limited interpretability, weak linkage between digital and molecular evidence, privacy constraints, and high validation and implementation costs. The following issue-challenge-future-need structure links each limitation to a specific research response and defines the evidence required for progress.
8.1. Issue #1 - fragmented single-modality evidence
Many studies optimize a model for one biomarker type, such as speech, handwriting, gait, or imaging. This approach simplifies acquisition and interpretation, but it can capture modality-specific nuisance variation and cannot represent all motor, non-motor, cognitive, and biological aspects of PD. Conversely, combining modalities is not automatically superior: complete-case selection may reduce sample size and introduce bias, while additional features increase overfitting and acquisition burden. The unresolved question is therefore whether a second modality provides reproducible incremental information beyond a strong clinical and unimodal baseline, not whether multimodal models achieve higher internal accuracy.
8.2. Challenge #1 - heterogeneous acquisition and incomplete modality overlap
Sensors, scanners, microphones, tasks, assays, sampling rates, medication states, and annotation rules differ across studies [26,29]. Multimodal cohorts additionally contain unequal visit schedules and missing inputs, so participants with complete data may not represent the target population. Naive pooling can therefore combine technical and biological variation, generate domain shift, and make model comparisons uninterpretable. Harmonisation must retain site and cohort provenance and must be fitted without access to held-out data.
8.3. Future need #1 - matched multimodal benchmarks and incremental-value testing
Future benchmarks should collect core behavioral, imaging, clinical, and molecular variables from matched participants across multiple centres while allowing transparent reporting of unavailable modalities [103,104]. Prespecified participant-level splits, hidden external test sets, modality ablations, missing-modality stress tests, calibration, subgroup results, and acquisition-cost analysis are required. Adaptive fusion should be compared with simpler early and late fusion under identical conditions [35,51]. A benchmark should reward transportability and clinical utility, not only peak accuracy.
8.4. Issue #2 - limited longitudinal and temporal evidence
Most PD AI studies use cross-sectional data and therefore estimate group differences at one time point rather than individual progression. Longitudinal resources such as PPMI and mPower provide repeated observations, but visit attrition, medication changes, irregular sampling, informative missingness, and practice or device effects complicate inference. Repeated visits also create leakage when observations from one participant appear in training and testing. Consequently, cross-sectional discrimination should not be described as progression prediction, and a time-aware model must be evaluated against clinically relevant future outcomes.
8.5. Challenge #2 - sparse, irregular, and treatment-dependent follow-up
Longitudinal labels may be missing, delayed, or measured at irregular intervals, and clinical scales contain measurement error and rater variability. Participants who discontinue follow-up may differ systematically from those retained. High-dimensional models can exploit visit timing, treatment status, or site schedules as shortcuts. These factors reduce effective sample size and can bias trajectories unless observation processes, censoring, treatment exposure, and within-participant correlation are modelled explicitly.
8.6. Future need #2 - prespecified time-aware and longitudinal validation
Future studies should use temporal holdouts, participant-level trajectory modelling, survival or joint longitudinal-event methods where appropriate, and evaluation at clinically meaningful forecast horizons [90,105]. Baselines should include current clinical scores, treatment status, and simple change models. Performance should report calibration over time, uncertainty, missingness sensitivity, and benefit for a defined monitoring or intervention decision. Model updating requires drift surveillance, locked version histories, and prospective revalidation; uncontrolled self-updating would undermine reproducibility and regulatory oversight.
8.7. Issue #3 - weak cross-dataset generalisation
Models that perform well within one dataset often lose accuracy or calibration when hardware, language, acquisition protocols, preprocessing, annotation, disease spectrum, or population composition changes [26,103,29]. Random internal splits do not measure this domain shift, especially when related participants, sites, or repeated recordings occur in both partitions. Cross-dataset performance should therefore be treated as a distinct endpoint. A model that requires extensive target-dataset tuning may be useful for adaptation research but has not demonstrated transportability to an unseen clinical setting.
8.8. Challenge #3 - demographic, clinical, and cohort bias
PD datasets often underrepresent racial and ethnic groups, lower-resource settings, prodromal disease, atypical parkinsonism, advanced disability, and participants with important comorbidities. Age, sex, language, education, socioeconomic status, treatment, and referral patterns can correlate with both labels and acquisition conditions. Aggregate performance can therefore conceal clinically important subgroup failure. Bias assessment must examine representation, label quality, missingness, calibration, and error rates within prespecified subgroups while recognising that very small subgroup samples yield uncertain estimates.
8.9. Future need #3 - external validation, harmonised reporting, and subgroup evaluation
Evaluation should include centre-, device-, language-, and cohort-held-out tests, followed by prospective multicentre validation when the intended use is clinical [50,51]. Reports should disclose participant flow, exclusions, prevalence, preprocessing, thresholds, calibration, confidence intervals, and subgroup results. Domain adaptation or self-supervised pretraining may improve transfer, but comparisons must include a no-adaptation baseline and must not use test labels for model selection. Harmonised reporting and shared evaluation code are preferable to forcing heterogeneous datasets into a single pooled distribution.
8.10. Issue #4 - limited clinical interpretability and trust
Deep models can represent high-dimensional imaging and multimodal data [35,29,30], but an explanation that highlights a feature or region does not establish biological causality, diagnostic validity, or clinical usefulness. Clinicians also require calibrated probabilities, uncertainty, failure-mode information, and a clear statement of the model's intended role. Interpretability is therefore broader than visual explanation: it includes traceable data provenance, clinically meaningful outputs, comparator performance, and evidence that users understand and act on the system appropriately [50,51].
8.11. Challenge #4 - accountability, human factors, and regulatory evidence
Opaque or overconfident predictions can promote automation bias and make error investigation difficult [41]. Responsibility for data quality, model updates, threshold selection, overrides, and adverse-event monitoring must be assigned before deployment. Regulatory evidence depends on a defined intended use, representative validation, risk management, cybersecurity, software version control, and post-deployment monitoring. A technically accurate model may still fail if it disrupts workflow, increases false referrals, or transfers decisions without adequate clinician oversight.
8.12. Future need #4 - explainable, uncertainty-aware, and workflow-tested AI
Future systems should combine calibrated prediction and uncertainty with explanations that are stable, clinically interpretable, and tested for user benefit [50,51]. Evaluation should include simulated and prospective workflow studies measuring decision quality, time, false reassurance, referral burden, override behaviour, and subgroup effects. Language models may assist with evidence retrieval or draft reporting, but generated content requires source grounding, privacy controls, human verification, and monitoring for unsupported statements [73]. Retrieval augmentation can reduce but not eliminate error; it should not convert an unvalidated predictive model into an autonomous clinical decision-maker.
8.13. Issue #5 - disconnect between digital phenotypes and molecular mechanisms
Patient-level digital models and molecular AI frequently address different endpoints in different cohorts. Speech, gait, handwriting, and imaging models estimate observable phenotype or clinical status, whereas protein, genetic, and compound models examine mechanisms or preclinical targets. Correlation between these levels cannot be inferred when participants, tissues, time points, and outcomes are unmatched. This separation limits biological interpretation of predictive features and prevents claims that a digital signature identifies a specific molecular mechanism.
8.14. Challenge #5 - cross-scale alignment, confounding, and causal interpretation
Phenotypic and molecular measurements differ in units, sampling frequency, tissue relevance, biological hierarchy, and nomenclature [10,106]. Disease duration, medication, age, ancestry, comorbidity, and batch effects may influence both levels and create non-causal associations. Matched multi-omics cohorts are typically smaller than unimodal datasets, while high dimensionality increases overfitting and multiple-testing risk. Cross-scale fusion therefore requires explicit causal assumptions, temporally aligned samples, assay-aware preprocessing, negative controls, and validation in independent cohorts.
8.15. Future need #5 - translational multiscale studies with mechanistic validation
Future work should link digital biomarkers, neuroimaging, clinical trajectories, genetics, biofluids, and molecular targets in matched longitudinal participants [104,106]. Analyses should prespecify whether the goal is prediction, mediation, subtyping, or target discovery; compare against clinical and single-scale baselines; and use nested feature selection, cohort-held-out validation, and sensitivity analyses for confounding. Candidate cross-scale relationships require replication and experimental or biological confirmation before they are described as mechanisms. Causal and representation-learning methods may help generate hypotheses, but they do not replace appropriate study design or therapeutic validation.
8.16. Issue #6 - privacy, governance, and data-sharing constraints
Clinical, imaging, wearable, genomic, and molecular data involve different consent, ownership, access, retention, and re-identification risks [19,49]. Centralising data may improve analysis but can conflict with participant expectations, institutional policy, data-use agreements, and jurisdiction-specific requirements. Privacy protection is not merely a technical barrier to be minimised; it is a condition of trustworthy research. Governance must define permitted uses, participant withdrawal, access review, breach response, secondary use, and responsibility for derived models.
8.17. Challenge #6 - institutional silos and non-identical local data
Data silos limit external validation and reduce diversity, but institutions also differ in case mix, coding, acquisition, labels, missingness, and clinical workflow. A distributed analysis can therefore fail even when raw data never move. Common data models and harmonised variables may improve comparability, yet they can conceal local meaning if provenance is removed. Collaborative studies should preserve site-level metadata, assess local label quality, and distinguish technical interoperability from clinical equivalence.
8.18. Future need #6 - governed privacy-preserving collaboration
Federated learning, secure multiparty computation, differential privacy, and encrypted aggregation can support collaboration without pooling all raw data [19,49], but they do not automatically guarantee privacy, fairness, or generalisation. Evaluation should include membership and reconstruction risk, malicious or unreliable clients, non-identically distributed data, communication cost, convergence, privacy-utility trade-offs, site-held-out performance, and subgroup calibration. A standard PD protocol should specify governance, consent compatibility, threat model, privacy budget where applicable, audit logs, model-update approval, and reproducible baselines. Privacy-preserving learning is therefore one component of a governed multicentre study, not a substitute for it.
8.19. Issue #7 - computational and operational scalability
PD AI pipelines may combine volumetric imaging, continuous sensor streams, speech, handwriting, clinical records, and molecular representations [17,105]. Their requirements differ: training a molecular or imaging foundation model is not equivalent to running an inference model on a wearable device. Claims of terabyte- or petabyte-scale need should be supported by the actual data unit and workflow. The relevant constraints are storage, transfer, memory, training time, inference latency, reliability, integration, and maintenance relative to the intended clinical or research task.
8.20. Challenge #7 - cost, sustainability, access, and maintenance
Specialised hardware, cloud services, data engineering, security, monitoring, and repeated validation create costs that may exclude smaller centres and lower-resource settings. Energy use is one consideration, but operational sustainability also includes staff expertise, vendor dependence, downtime, software updates, cybersecurity, and the cost of false alerts. A model with marginally higher accuracy may provide less value if it is difficult to deploy, recalibrate, or support. Resource use should therefore be assessed alongside clinical benefit and equity.
8.21. Future need #7 - cost-aware and deployment-tested AI
Future studies should report parameter count, training and inference time, memory, energy or compute estimates where feasible, hardware, latency, failure recovery, and maintenance requirements [105]. Lightweight architectures, pruning, quantisation, knowledge distillation, and edge inference may support wearable monitoring [16,[52], [53], [54], [55], [56]], but compression must be evaluated for calibration, subgroup performance, robustness, and battery or connectivity constraints. Prospective workflow testing should compare local, edge, and cloud deployment against simpler alternatives and measure total cost per clinically useful decision. Scalability should mean reliable service across representative settings, not only the ability to process more data.
9. Limitations of the review
This review is limited by heterogeneity in study populations, diagnostic criteria, modality definitions, preprocessing, outcome measures, and validation protocols. The evidence base is vulnerable to publication and selective-reporting bias because positive model results and peak metrics may be reported more often than negative findings, calibration failures, or unsuccessful external tests. The review also depends on information available in the cited reports; missing participant-level details, incomplete subgroup data, and inconsistent reporting prevented formal meta-analysis and direct ranking of algorithms.
The literature search and screening process may not capture every eligible study, particularly non-English, non-indexed, recently released, or rapidly updated AI work. Public-dataset and database characteristics can change, and repeated publications from overlapping cohorts may reduce the effective independence of the evidence. Molecular screening outcomes, retrospective diagnostic performance, and conceptual frameworks were therefore interpreted separately. These limitations mean that the review identifies patterns and priorities but cannot establish pooled diagnostic accuracy, comparative superiority, or clinical effectiveness.
10. Conclusion
This review synthesises AI research across speech, gait and wearables, handwriting, neuroimaging, clinical measures, genetics, biofluids, molecular modelling, and drug-discovery-related tasks in PD. The available evidence demonstrates technical feasibility for classification, symptom monitoring, severity estimation, progression research, and preclinical prioritisation. It does not establish that any model family or multimodal framework is ready for autonomous diagnosis or routine clinical deployment. Conclusions must remain conditional on cohort composition, sampling unit, preprocessing, validation design, endpoint definition, and external testing.
Conventional ML can be efficient and interpretable when datasets are modest and features are clinically motivated, while deep learning can represent images, raw signals, sequences, and molecular graphs with less manual feature engineering. Neither is consistently superior across heterogeneous studies. Multimodal fusion may add complementary information, but it also increases missing-data bias, computation, and acquisition burden. Its value must be demonstrated against strong clinical and unimodal baselines using matched participants, ablations, calibration, and external validation.
The most consistent weaknesses in the reviewed literature are small or selective cohorts, repeated-measure leakage, class imbalance, inconsistent endpoints, incomplete statistical reporting, limited external and prospective validation, weak calibration, and insufficient subgroup analysis. Fig. 11 is an illustrative workflow that organises these considerations; it is not evidence that feature fusion or feedback optimisation resolves them. Clinical translation additionally depends on interpretability, uncertainty, privacy, regulation, workflow integration, cost, and post-deployment monitoring.
The gap between digital phenotypes and molecular mechanisms remains substantial because these data are rarely measured in matched longitudinal participants and predictive association does not establish causality. Federated and privacy-preserving methods may enable multicentre analysis, but they cannot correct biased recruitment, inconsistent labels, or domain shift. The strongest near-term priorities are standardised reporting, participant-level and time-aware evaluation, representative multicentre cohorts, independent external tests, explainable and uncertainty-aware decision support, multimodal missing-data analysis, and prospective validation for a clearly defined clinical task.
In conclusion, current evidence supports AI as a research and adjunctive decision-support tool for selected PD-related tasks, with the level of support varying substantially by modality and validation design. Claims of clinical readiness should be reserved for models that demonstrate reproducible incremental value, calibration, subgroup reliability, usability, and safety in independent prospective settings. The principal contribution of this review is a dataset- and modality-aware critical synthesis that connects model choice with validation risk, clinical role, multiscale evidence, and concrete research priorities, rather than an assertion that a single unified architecture has already solved PD assessment.
